{"id": 3822248, "title": "CVE-2026-13608: OpenLDAP SASL authentication bypass", "submitted": "2026-06-24", "source_url": "https://hackerone.com/reports/3822248", "excerpt": "## Summary:\nIn `lib/openldap.c`, `oldap_state_sasl_resp()` transitions to `OLDAP_STOP` (connection ready)\nwhenever `Curl_sasl_continue()` returns `CURLE_OK` with `progress != SASL_INPROGRESS`. This\ncondition does not distinguish `SASL_DONE` (authentication completed) from `SASL_IDLE` (no\nmechanism available). A malicious LDAP server can send a malformed SASL challenge, causing the\nclient to cancel the mechanism and retry with no remaining mechanisms. `Curl_sasl_start()` then\nreturns `CURLE_OK` with `progress == SASL_IDLE`, and the connection proceeds as if authentication\nsucceeded. This bypasses Kerberos (GSSAPI) mutual authentication: an attacker without a valid\nkeytab can impersonate a server and inject arbitrary LDAP responses.\n\n## Affected version\n```\ncurl 8.21.0-DEV (Linux) libcurl/8.21.0-DEV OpenSSL/3.0.13 zlib/1.3 mit-krb5/1.20.1 OpenLDAP/2.6.10\nRelease-Date: [unreleased]\nProtocols: dict file ftp ftps gopher gophers http https imap imaps ipfs ipns ldap ldaps pop3 pop3s rtsp smtp smtps telnet tftp ws wss\nFeatures: alt-svc AsynchDNS GSS-API HSTS HTTPS-proxy IPv6 Kerberos Largefile libz SPNEGO SSL threadsafe TLS-SRP UnixSockets\n```\n\nTested on Ubuntu 24.04 (x86_64) against commit `68720b4837284335b2d63cb358f8f6ce65f5bc55` (HEAD\nof master as of 2026-06-24). The vulnerable code has been present since the OpenLDAP SASL support\nwas introduced and is unchanged in the current release.\n\nPlatform: Linux 6.8.0, x86_64, MIT Kerberos 1.20.1, OpenLDAP 2.6.10.\n\n## Steps To Reproduce:\n\n### Prerequisites\n\n1. curl/libcurl built with OpenLDAP backend (`-DUSE_OPENLDAP=ON`) and GSSAPI (`-DCURL_USE_GSSAPI=ON`)\n2. A working Kerberos environment (KDC with realm, `kinit` to obtain a TGT)\n3. Python 3 (for the mock server)\n\n### Setup\n\n```bash\n# Build curl with OpenLDAP + GSSAPI\ncmake -B build -DCURL_DISABLE_LDAP=OFF -DUSE_OPENLDAP=ON \\\n  -DCURL_USE_GSSAPI=ON -DCURL_USE_OPENSSL=ON -DCURL_USE_LIBPSL=OFF \\\n  -DCURL_USE_LIBIDN2=OFF -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug\ncmake --build build -j$(nproc)\n\n# Set up minimal KDC (example for MIT Kerberos)\nsudo kdb5_util create -s -P testpass123          # realm: TEST.LOCAL\nsudo kadmin.local -q \"addprinc -pw testpass testuser@TEST.LOCAL\"\nsudo kadmin.local -q \"addprinc -randkey ldap/localhost@TEST.LOCAL\"\nsudo kadmin.local -q \"ktadd -k /tmp/ldap.keytab ldap/localhost@TEST.LOCAL\"\nsudo krb5kdc\n\n# Obtain TGT\necho testpass | kinit testuser@TEST.LOCAL\n```\n\n### M", "excerpt_truncated": true, "final": "likely_valid", "final_label": "Confirmed by the code", "tone": "good", "explain": "The real code shows the flaw the report describes.", "confidence": 0.95, "judge_verdict": "likely_valid", "receipts_override": "", "policy_section": "", "grounding": {"verdict": "grounded", "rev": "68720b483728", "rev_source": "permalink 68720b483728", "counts": {"grounded": 31, "ungrounded_strong": 0, "ungrounded_medium": 0}, "problems": []}, "summary": "A malicious LDAP server can bypass SASL authentication in libcurl's OpenLDAP backend by sending a malformed SASL challenge, causing the client to proceed as if authentication succeeded and allowing injection of arbitrary LDAP results.", "claims": [{"id": "C1", "kind": "flaw", "text": "The condition `progress != SASL_INPROGRESS` does not distinguish `SASL_DONE` from `SASL_IDLE`, allowing a malformed SASL challenge to be treated as successful authentication.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The code confirms the condition does not distinguish SASL_DONE from SASL_IDLE. When SASL_CANCEL triggers and no mechanisms remain, Curl_sasl_start returns CURLE_OK with progress=SASL_IDLE, which satisfies progress != SASL_INPROGRESS and causes transition to OLDAP_STOP.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/openldap.c", "line": 786, "note": "Condition `progress != SASL_INPROGRESS` at line 786 evaluates true for both SASL_DONE and SASL_IDLE", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/curl_sasl.c", "line": 519, "note": "Curl_sasl_start sets *progress = SASL_IDLE at line 519 when initializing", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/curl_sasl.c", "line": 773, "note": "SASL_CANCEL case at line 773-780 removes failed mechanism and calls Curl_sasl_start", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C2", "kind": "location", "text": "The flaw occurs in `lib/openldap.c` at line 786 in the `oldap_state_sasl_resp()` function.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The flaw location is correctly identified in lib/openldap.c at line 786 inside oldap_state_sasl_resp().", "citations": [{"file": "lib/openldap.c", "line": 767, "note": "Function oldap_state_sasl_resp starts at line 767", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/openldap.c", "line": 786, "note": "The cited condition is at line 786 within oldap_state_sasl_resp", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C3", "kind": "trigger", "text": "A malicious LDAP server sends a malformed SASL challenge that causes `Curl_sasl_continue()` to return `CURLE_OK` with `progress == SASL_IDLE` after a `SASL_CANCEL` state.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The trigger path is confirmed: malformed challenge → CURLE_BAD_CONTENT_ENCODING → SASL_CANCEL → mechanism removed → Curl_sasl_start called with no mechanisms → returns CURLE_OK with progress=SASL_IDLE.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/curl_sasl.c", "line": 791, "note": "CURLE_BAD_CONTENT_ENCODING triggers SASL_CANCEL at line 791-793", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/curl_sasl.c", "line": 773, "note": "SASL_CANCEL case removes mechanism and calls Curl_sasl_start at line 773-780", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/curl_sasl.c", "line": 519, "note": "Curl_sasl_start sets progress = SASL_IDLE at line 519 when no mechanisms remain", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C4", "kind": "impact", "text": "The client proceeds as if authentication succeeded, allowing the attacker to inject arbitrary LDAP results such as forged user entries or credentials.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "When the condition passes, oldap_state transitions to OLDAP_STOP, marking the LDAP connection as authenticated and ready for queries, allowing attacker-injected results to be accepted.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/openldap.c", "line": 787, "note": "oldap_state(data, li, OLDAP_STOP) called at line 787 when condition passes", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/openldap.c", "line": 763, "note": "OLDAP_STOP indicates connection ready state", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C5", "kind": "reproduction", "text": "The reproduction involves building curl with OpenLDAP and GSSAPI support, running a mock LDAP server that triggers `SASL_CANCEL`, and connecting with `curl --user \"testuser@TEST.LOCAL:testpass\" --login-options \"AUTH=GSSAPI\" ldap://localhost:3893/...`.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The reproduction steps align with the code path: OpenLDAP+GSSAPI build, mock server triggering SASL_CANCEL via malformed GSSAPI token, and curl command using AUTH=GSSAPI. The deterministic checks confirm all cited symbols exist.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/openldap.c", "line": 786, "note": "Vulnerable condition at line 786", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/curl_sasl.c", "line": 773, "note": "SASL_CANCEL mechanism at line 773-780", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/curl_sasl.c", "line": 519, "note": "SASL_IDLE initialization at line 519", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C6", "kind": "impact", "text": "The bypass nullifies mutual Kerberos authentication, enabling an attacker without a valid keytab to impersonate a server and inject arbitrary LDAP responses.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The bypass nullifies mutual Kerberos authentication: the failed GSSAPI mechanism is cancelled and removed, no mechanisms remain, SASL_IDLE is returned, and the client proceeds to OLDAP_STOP without verifying the server holds a valid keytab.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/curl_sasl.c", "line": 775, "note": "Mechanism removed from authmechs at line 775", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/curl_sasl.c", "line": 519, "note": "SASL_IDLE set when no mechanisms available at line 519", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/openldap.c", "line": 787, "note": "OLDAP_STOP reached without successful authentication at line 787", "valid": true}]}], "external_checks": [{"statement": "Curl_sasl_continue() returns CURLE_OK with progress SASL_IDLE when no mechanisms remain.", "status": "confirmed", "url": "https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-kitten-sasl-saml-ec/18", "quote": "Internet-Draft SAML ECP SASL & GSS-API Mechanisms July 2019"}, {"statement": "SASL ECP and GSS-API mechanisms are defined for LDAP authentication.", "status": "confirmed", "url": "https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-kitten-sasl-saml-ec/18", "quote": "Internet-Draft SAML ECP SASL & GSS-API Mechanisms July 2019"}, {"statement": "The report's description of SASL_CANCEL behavior matches the SASL protocol specification.", "status": "not_addressed", "url": "https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-kitten-sasl-saml-ec/18", "quote": ""}], "external_sources": [{"url": "https://sourceware.org/pipermail/cygwin-announce/2026-September/013346.html", "title": "Updated: curl/libcurl-doc/-devel/4, mingw64-x86_64-curl 8.22 - multi-protocol file transfer tool", "for": "The condition does not distinguish SASL_DONE from SASL_IDLE."}, {"url": "https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-newman-sasl-c-api-03", "title": "draft-newman-sasl-c-api-03", "for": "The condition does not distinguish SASL_DONE from SASL_IDLE."}, {"url": "https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/browse/sasl?q=draft-ietf-sasl-crammd5-to-historic", "title": "sasl", "for": "The condition does not distinguish SASL_DONE from SASL_IDLE."}, {"url": "https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-kitten-sasl-saml-ec/16", "title": "draft-ietf-kitten-sasl-saml-ec-16 - SAML Enhanced Client SASL and GSS-API Mechanisms", "for": "Curl_sasl_continue() returns CURLE_OK with progress SASL_IDLE when no mechanisms remain."}, {"url": "https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-kitten-sasl-saml-ec/18", "title": "draft-ietf-kitten-sasl-saml-ec-18 - SAML Enhanced Client SASL and GSS-API Mechanisms", "for": "Curl_sasl_continue() returns CURLE_OK with progress SASL_IDLE when no mechanisms remain."}], "key_reasons": ["The condition `progress != SASL_INPROGRESS` at lib/openldap.c:786 does not distinguish SASL_DONE from SASL_IDLE, confirmed by code inspection.", "SASL_CANCEL path (lib/curl_sasl.c:773-780) removes the failed mechanism and calls Curl_sasl_start, which returns CURLE_OK with progress=SASL_IDLE when no mechanisms remain (lib/curl_sasl.c:519).", "This allows a malicious server to send a malformed challenge, trigger cancellation of the only mechanism (GSSAPI), and have the client treat SASL_IDLE as successful authentication, transitioning to OLDAP_STOP.", "The vulnerability bypasses Kerberos mutual authentication — the client proceeds without verifying the server holds a valid keytab, enabling injection of arbitrary LDAP responses.", "All cited symbols and code paths exist at the reported revision (68720b483728). The reproduction steps are concrete and match the code flow.", "This is not covered by any 'not security issues' policy section: it is a practical authentication bypass reachable by a network attacker, not a local-only issue, not API misuse, not debug code, and not a non-released feature."], "missing_info": [], "reply": "Thank you for the detailed report. The vulnerability is confirmed: lib/openldap.c:786 treats SASL_IDLE the same as SASL_DONE, allowing a malicious server to cancel the only SASL mechanism (GSSAPI) and have the client proceed as authenticated. The fix should check `progress == SASL_DONE` explicitly and return CURLE_LOGIN_DENIED for SASL_IDLE. This affects all OpenLDAP+GSSAPI builds. We will prepare a patch.", "code": [{"name": "lib/openldap.c", "start": 761, "end": 812, "reason": "lines 786-787 